   12 | . ./brace.sh
      | \__________/
loading: warning[load-carriage-withheld-under-unknown-cwd]: [unwritten:
load-carriage-withheld-under-unknown-cwd]
      [ book.sh:14:1
   14 | . ./ifc.sh
      | \________/
#!/bin/sh
# dorc probe (read-only): checks per-SITE convergence, mutates nothing.
# When run, emits one results-record per site on stdout (the return channel):
#   site <leafid> effect=<holds|absent|cant-tell> rc=<n>
# effect is derived from the probe command's rc (0=holds, 1=absent, else cant-tell);
# rc is the raw PROBE-command status (opaque to Dorc -- the record is the out-of-band lane).

printf 'dorc-records/1 nonce=dorc attempt=1 host=localhost book=e0e50ea001cd07cab5a9bf3171f4e5aaf948b6399d46d5c1911ac4ed453a5535 sites=0 @@dorc@@\n'
# site:0 unresolvable-no-probe
# site:1 unresolvable-no-probe
# site:2 unresolvable-no-probe
# site:3 unresolvable-no-probe
# site:4 unresolvable-no-probe
# site:5 unresolvable-no-probe
# site:6 unresolvable-no-probe
printf 'dorc-records-end/1 nonce=dorc @@dorc@@\n'
probe: note[site-unresolvable]: sm 3 sites run unprobed (no read-only check
could be shipped): `. ./sub.sh`, `. ./brace.sh`, `. ./ifc.sh` -- run `dorc why`
for the per-site detail (the apply runs each anyway, to stay safe)
      [ book.sh:10:1
   10 | . ./sub.sh
      | \________/

   = help:  sm site runs `. ./sub.sh`
hint: '.' (line 10) is unmodeled: it is the first wall -- an oracle vouching its convergence would elide it when converged; 2 more unmodeled walls -- dorc why
dorc: run `dorc why` for the per-site cause-chains, or `dorc why book.sh:N` to query a source line
#!/bin/sh
# dorc apply: the book, with already-converged/dead lines elided (value-preserving stand-in).

#!/bin/sh
# floor30-atlas-return-inside-compound-in-sourced-file — QUESTION: at a sourced file's top level, a
# `return` inside `( … )`, inside `{ … }`, and inside `if … fi` — which of the three leave the FILE,
# and which leave only the enclosing subshell?
#
# Why the inclusion design needs it: the paste-exclusion set is stated as "a top-level `return`", and
# whether a `return` nested one compound deep still counts as top-level is precisely the boundary a
# scanner would have to draw. Three separate sourced files, so a cell that ends its own file cannot
# hide the next: a missing `*-file-tail` line IS that cell's answer.
. ./sub.sh
printf 'book: after-sub-file status=[%s]\n' "$?"
. ./brace.sh
printf 'book: after-brace-file status=[%s]\n' "$?"
. ./ifc.sh
printf 'book: after-if-file status=[%s]\n' "$?"
printf 'book-tail\n'
